    </div><div class="prose-slot" lang="en"><p>Paris is located in northern central France, in a north-bending arc of the river <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seine">Seine</a>, whose crest includes two islands, the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%8Ele_Saint-Louis">Île Saint-Louis</a> and the larger <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%8Ele_de_la_Cit%C3%A9">Île de la Cité</a>, which form the oldest part of Paris. The river's mouth on the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/English_Channel">English Channel</a> (<i>La Manche</i>) is about 233&#160;mi (375&#160;km) downstream from Paris. Paris is spread widely on both banks of the river. Overall, Paris is relatively flat, and the lowest point is 35&#160;m (115&#160;ft) <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Above_mean_sea_level">above sea level</a>. Paris has several prominent hills, the highest of which is <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Montmartre">Montmartre</a> at 130&#160;m (427&#160;ft).</p><a class="read" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paris#Geography">Geography ↗</a></div><div class="topic-articles"><div class="topic-article">
    <div class="body">
        <p class="source"><img src="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Wikipedia-logo-v2.svg?width=60" alt="" width="18"> en.wikipedia.org</p>
        <a class="title" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geography_of_Paris" lang="en">Geography of Paris</a><p class="desc">overview of the geography of Paris</p></div><img class="pic k-photo"
     style="--a:1.1921"
     src="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Paris%20by%20Sentinel-2.jpg?width=330"
     alt=""
     loading="lazy"><span class="tools"><button class="info"
            type="button"
            popovertarget="p16"
            aria-label="Credit">i</button><span id="p16" popover class="pop">Copernicus Sentinel-2, ESA · CC BY-SA 3.0 igo</span></span></div></div></section>
